          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Milnor64"} Milnor 1964
            introduced the notion of the
            %em link group
            as a way to study
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/links"} links
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"} knots
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_n S^1 \to M
            such that the components have disjoint images. Similarly, two links are
            %strong homotopic
            if there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            between the maps which is a link at every time. Thus links can be deformed in such a manner that individual components can pass through themselves, but not through other components. Also link components can have self-intersections or the map on a component can be a constant map. Milnor uses the term
            %strong proper link
            to refer to a link in which the map is a homeomorphism onto its image.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+link"} Whitehead link
            is a simple example of a link that is not trivial under ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopy
            but is trivial under Milnor’s notion of homotopy.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariants"} invariants
            come from explicit descriptions of the link groups of particular links. Specifically, Milnor calls a link
            %em almost trivial
            if every proper sublink is trivial (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brunnian+link"} Brunnian link
          %h2#link_group Link Group
          %p
            Let us begin by describing the link group. Milnor’s alternative description is as follows. Consider the complement of a link
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            in an open
            = succeed "-manifold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basepoint"} basepoint
            in this complement and so have the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            are equivalent if the link
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ∪
                  %msup
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \cup \alpha^{-1} \beta
            is homotopic in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            to one of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∪
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L' \cup 1
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the constant loop at the basepoint). The
            %strong link group
            is the group of equivalence classes of such loops.
          %p A more practical description is the following.
          #linkgroup.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
