                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-2
          #muinvs.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_milnor_section_5
              Theorem (
              %a{:href => "#jmLinkGroups"}> Milnor, Section 5
              )
            %p
              The integers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 1
                    %mi ⋯
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(i_1 \cdots i_{n-2}, n_1 n)
              are homotopy invariants of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is completely specified by these integers.
          %p There was nothing special about the choice of components. A similar procedure works for any pair of components. The resulting integers obey the following rules:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi μ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 2
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi μ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mi n
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 2
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 3
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi μ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 1
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mi ν
                          %mi r
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mn 1
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mi ν
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                          %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mi ν
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mi μ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi h
                            %mn 1
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi h
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                          %mi r
                          %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\mu(i_1 i_2 \cdots i_{n-2}, i_{n-1} i_n) &amp;= \mu(i_n i_1 i_2 \cdots i_{n-3}, i_{n-2} i_{n-2}) \\
                  \\mu(i_1 \cdots i_{\nu} r j_1 \cdots j_{n-\nu-2} s) &amp;= (-1)^{n-\nu} \sum \mu(h_1 \cdots h_{n-2} r s)
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            In the second identity, the summation is over all shuffle products of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i_1 \cdots i_{\nu})
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mn 1
                    %mi ⋯
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi ν
                        %mo −
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (j_1 \cdots j_{n - \nu - 2})
          %p
            Let us expand on the definition of the
            = succeed "-invariants." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "-manifold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \mathcal{G}(L)
            be the integral group ring of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}(L)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_i(L)
            is a product of powers of conjugates of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi α
                    %mi i
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i^s
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi J
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in J \mathcal{G}(L)
            by interpreting:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi α
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo +
                              %mi y
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msubsup
                            %mi α
                            %mi i
                            %mi x
                          %msubsup
                            %mi α
                            %mi i
                            %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi α
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi k
                              %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi α
                            %mi i
                            %mi x
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi k
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi α
                            %mi i
                            %mi β
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi β
                          %mi α
                          %msup
                            %mi β
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\alpha_i^{x + y} &amp;= \alpha_i^x \alpha_i^y \\
                  \\alpha_i^{k x} &amp;= (\alpha_i^x)^k \\
                  \\alpha_i^\beta &amp;= \beta \alpha \beta^{-1}
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi J
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in J\mathcal{G}(L)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \in \mathcal{G}(L)
          %p
            We write the kernel of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi J
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \mathcal{G}(L) \to J \mathcal{G}(L^i)
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒦
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_i(L)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi J
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{R}(L) \coloneqq J \mathcal{G}(L) / \mathcal{K}_1(L)^2 + \cdots + \mathcal{K}_n(L)^2
          %p
            Now the notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi α
                    %mi i
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i^s
            for an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_i(L)
            does not provide an injective map from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J\mathcal{G}(L)
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_i(L)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_i(L) + (\mathcal{K}_1(L)^2 + \cdots + \mathcal{K}_n(L)^2)
            which is naturally isomorphic to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}(L^i)
          #trivlink.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_milnor_theorem_5
              Theorem (
              %a{:href => "#jmLinkGroups"}> Milnor, Theorem 5
              )
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              be a link which is homotopic to one in with the
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_i(L)
              can be expressed uniquely in the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                      %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i^\sigma
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi L
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in \mathcal{R}(L^i)
          %p
            Now let us suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is trivial. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(L)
            is the free product of the fundamental group of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with the infinite cyclic groups generated by the (elements representing the) meridians of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_n
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i = a_i - 1
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J G(L)
            %strong canonical word
            to be a product of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0 k_{j_1} \phi_1 k_{j_2} \phi_2 \cdots k_{j_p} \phi_p
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \ge 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_i \in \pi_1(M)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \le j_i \le n
            %strong canonical sentence
            is a sum or difference of any number of canonical words. It turns out (
            %a{:href => "#jmLinkGroups"}> Milnor, Theorem 7
            ) that each element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}(L)
            is represented by a unique canonical sentence.
          %p
            Now let us return to the case of the almost trivial link in Euclidean space. From above, we have the element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta'_n \in \mathcal{A}_i(L^n)
            corresponding to the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta'_n
            since removing, say, the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^i
            which is homotopic to the trivial link on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
            components. Removing the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            component corresponds to setting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J G(L^n)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i = 0
            we must have that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta'_n \mapsto 1
            and thus (by uniqueness)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ↦
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \mapsto 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i
            divides
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 1
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_{i_1} \cdots k_{i_{n-2}}
            for some permutation of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:muinvs"} (1)
          %p
            Now, how do we interpret or calculate these invariants? We need to work out what an expression of the form in
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:muinvs"} (1)
            is saying. Consider a canonical word,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mn 1
                    %mi ⋯
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_{i_1} \cdots k_{i_{n-2}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 1
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\alpha_{n-1}^{k_{i_1} \cdots k_{i_{n-2}}}
          %p
            Let us write
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha = \alpha_{n-1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^{k_1}
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mi α
                    %msubsup
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^{a_1 - 1} = a_1 \alpha a_1^{-1} \alpha^{-1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{n-1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{n-1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{n-1}
            with the loop so far constructed.
          %p So the general method is as follows: choose two components of the link. Write one of them as a word in the meridians of the others. Then simplify this word using the other chosen link as the “base”: namely, write everything in terms of conjugates of that base. This will then separate out into the desired form and, hopefully, the link invariants can be read off.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Milnor64
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Milnor"}> John Milnor
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Link groups
                %strong 2
                59 (1954) 177-195 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1969685"}> doi:10.2307/1969685
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1969685"} jstor:1969685
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=71020"} MR
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Link_group"} Link group
